digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Motivated by the likelihood that additional and especially enzymatic phage counter-defenses remain undiscovered, the paper investigates T4/Tevenvirinae resistance to the DarTG1 DNA ADP-ribosylation defense and develops genetic, comparative, and biochemical evidence to test and characterize a conserved phage NADAR protein, AdfN, as an enzymatic anti-DarTG1 factor that removes DarT1-mediated ADP-ribose modifications from DNA.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a bacterial anti-phage defense blocks replication through a specific DNA modification, unexplained conserved phage resistance can be investigated by combining genetic mapping, comparative conservation, mechanistic hypotheses, and biochemical tests to identify and characterize the responsible counter-defense."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, many bacterial anti-phage defense systems have been discovered recently."];
  "n10" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction rule: If a class of mechanisms may be hard to characterize rather than rare, then targeted mechanistic studies are needed to uncover and characterize members of that class."];
  "n11" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently it is unclear whether enzymatic counter-defenses are rare or hard to characterize, and if difficulty is possible then targeted mechanistic work is needed, so mechanistic investigation of candidate enzymatic counter-defenses is warranted."];
  "n14" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, ADP-ribosylated DNA cannot be replicated (and sometimes not transcribed), thereby preventing phage replication."];
  "n15" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction rule: If a bacterial defense blocks phage replication by modifying phage DNA, then successful phages must evade that modification or reverse its effect to replicate."];
  "n16" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently DarTG blocks replication via ADP-ribosylated DNA, and if replication is blocked then phages must evade or reverse the modification, so phages are expected to evolve anti-DarTG counter-defenses."];
  "n18" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction knowledge: If a defense system is potent and known evasions exist, then unexplained resistance in some phages is plausibly due to additional, unknown counter-defense factors."];
  "n19" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, T4 encodes an active AdfA allele, but T4 ΔadfA remains resistant to DarTG1."];
  "n20" [label="(25,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently T4 remains DarTG1-resistant even without AdfA, and generally unexplained resistance suggests an additional factor, so T4 likely encodes a second, unknown anti-DarTG1 factor."];
  "n21" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, AdfA (RB69) and AdfB (ICP1) were identified as anti-DarTG counter-defenses via escape mutant analyses."];
  "n23" [label="(0,0,0) Induction background: If multiple known anti-DarTG factors are small, phage-specific, and act by binding/inhibiting DarT, then newly found anti-DarTG factors might also appear phage-encoded and may be discoverable genetically by mapping resistance loci."];
  "n24" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently AdfA/AdfB are small interaction-based inhibitors discovered by genetics, and generally similar counter-defenses can be found by mapping resistance, so genetic mapping/co-infection approaches are suitable to find additional anti-DarTG factors."];
  "n25" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, across an expanded Tevenvirinae set, AdfA presence or allele type does not correlate with DarTG1 susceptibility."];
  "n27" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction knowledge: If a phenotype (DarTG1 resistance) is broadly conserved in a clade but does not track a candidate gene (AdfA), then another conserved genetic element likely explains the phenotype."];
  "n28" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently Tevenvirinae resistance is widespread while AdfA does not correlate, and generally a conserved phenotype implies a conserved cause, so a conserved non-AdfA element likely confers DarTG1 resistance in T-even phages."];
  "n29" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, DarTG1 uses a NADAR-superfamily antitoxin (DarG1), whereas DarTG2 uses a macrodomain antitoxin (DarG2) that acts as an ADP-ribose eraser."];
  "n3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction background: If both bacterial defenses and phage counter-defenses are rapidly being discovered, then an ongoing arms race likely produces additional, still-unknown counter-defense mechanisms worth identifying."];
  "n31" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction knowledge: If NADAR proteins can serve as antitoxins/erasers in bacteria, then a phage-encoded NADAR-like protein could plausibly function enzymatically to remove ADP-ribose from DNA and thereby counter DarT1."];
  "n32" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently DarTG1 involves a NADAR antitoxin and DarG1 interaction is unclear, and generally NADAR proteins could act as enzymatic erasers, so a plausible hypothesis is that phages encode a NADAR protein that enzymatically reverses DarT1-mediated DNA ADP-ribosylation."];
  "n34" [label="(29,0,0) Currently, co-infection of sensitive and resistant phages plus characterization of chimeras identified a second anti-DarTG1 element conserved in T-even phages."];
  "n35" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction rule: If resistance maps to a conserved genetic element identified by recombination/chimeras, then that element is a strong candidate causal anti-defense factor to test for necessity and sufficiency."];
  "n36" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently chimeras map resistance to a conserved element, and if mapped then it is a strong causal candidate, so this conserved element should be defined and experimentally tested as the anti-DarTG1 factor."];
  "n38" [label="(31,0,0) Currently, AdfN_T4 is a NADAR protein (same superfamily as DarG1) and has enzymatic activity removing ADP-ribose from DNA."];
  "n4" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently there is rapid discovery of defenses and counter-defenses, and generally if such an arms-race is active then more undiscovered counter-defenses likely exist, so it is reasonable to search for new counter-defense factors."];
  "n41" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction rule: If a phage encodes an enzyme that removes the defensive DNA modification that blocks replication, then that enzyme constitutes an enzymatic counter-defense mechanism enabling resistance."];
  "n42" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently AdfN (a NADAR enzyme) removes ADP-ribose from DNA, and if removing the modification negates the defense, then AdfN is an enzymatic anti-DarTG1 counter-defense."];
  "n6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, relatively fewer enzymatic counter-defense proteins have been identified than non-enzymatic ones."];
  "n7" [label="(0,0,0) Induction background: If enzymatic counter-defenses are underrepresented among identified mechanisms, then a plausible generalization is that enzymatic counter-defenses may be understudied/hidden and additional enzymatic counter-defenses remain to be discovered."];
  "n8" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently enzymatic counter-defenses are fewer than non-enzymatic ones, and generally underrepresentation suggests missing discoveries, so additional enzymatic counter-defenses are likely to exist."];
  "n9" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, it is unclear whether enzymatic counter-defenses are truly rare or merely hard to characterize."];
  "n1" -> "n4" [label="induction-case"];
  "n3" -> "n4" [label="induction-common"];
  "n6" -> "n8" [label="induction-case"];
  "n7" -> "n8" [label="induction-common"];
